WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.272 --> 00:00:04.292
- Good morning Believe Nation.

00:00:04.292 --> 00:00:05.580
My name is Evan Carmichael.

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:06.939
My one word is believe.

00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:08.798
And I believe that
entrepreneurs will solve

00:00:08.798 --> 00:00:10.661
all the major problems of the world.

00:00:10.661 --> 00:00:12.633
I started the Believe Life
series to try to focus

00:00:12.633 --> 00:00:15.597
on other topics, not just
related to entrepreneurship,

00:00:15.597 --> 00:00:18.341
but useful skills, ideas and strategies

00:00:18.341 --> 00:00:20.472
to help you live a better life.

00:00:20.472 --> 00:00:22.088
So today we're going to
talk about how you can

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:23.171
detect a lie.

00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:25.829
- Everybody lies.

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:28.890
- And as I was, guys, as
you're listening, if you hear

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.288
something that really inspires you,

00:00:30.288 --> 00:00:32.265
please put it down in the comments below

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:33.612
and put quotes around it, so other people

00:00:33.612 --> 00:00:35.104
can be inspired as well.

00:00:35.104 --> 00:00:36.492
And when you write it down,

00:00:36.492 --> 00:00:39.662
it's much more likely to
stick with yourself, as well.

00:00:39.662 --> 00:00:40.495
Enjoy.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:01.555
- Lying has evolutionary
value to us as species.

00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:04.425
Researchers have long known
that the more intelligent

00:01:04.425 --> 00:01:07.108
the species, the larger the
neocortex, the more likely

00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:09.329
it is to be deceptive.

00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:11.107
Now you might remember Koko.

00:01:11.107 --> 00:01:13.121
Does anybody here
remember Koko the Gorilla,

00:01:13.121 --> 00:01:14.307
who was taught sign language.

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:17.275
Koko was taught to
communicate vie sign language.

00:01:17.275 --> 00:01:19.432
Here's Koko with her
kitten, sort of cute, little

00:01:19.432 --> 00:01:21.406
fluffy pet kitten.

00:01:21.406 --> 00:01:24.737
Koko once blamed her pet
kitten for ripping a sink

00:01:24.737 --> 00:01:25.969
out of the wall.

00:01:25.969 --> 00:01:27.739
(laughter)

00:01:27.739 --> 00:01:30.318
We're hard-wired to become
leaders of the pack.

00:01:30.318 --> 00:01:32.265
It starts really, really early.

00:01:32.265 --> 00:01:33.563
How early?

00:01:33.563 --> 00:01:36.453
Well, babies will fake a cry, pause,

00:01:36.453 --> 00:01:39.492
wait to see who's coming and
then go right back to crying.

00:01:39.492 --> 00:01:40.809
(laughter)

00:01:40.809 --> 00:01:44.119
One-year-olds learn concealment.

00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:46.291
Two-year-olds bluff.

00:01:46.291 --> 00:01:48.058
Five-year-olds lie outright.

00:01:48.058 --> 00:01:50.225
They manipulate via flattery.

00:01:50.225 --> 00:01:52.739
Nine-year-olds, masters of the cover-up.

00:01:52.739 --> 00:01:54.337
By the time you enter
college, you're going to lie

00:01:54.337 --> 00:01:58.018
to your Mom in one out of
every five interactions.

00:01:58.018 --> 00:02:00.327
By the time we enter this work world,

00:02:00.327 --> 00:02:01.812
and we're breadwinners, we enter a world

00:02:01.812 --> 00:02:05.796
that is just cluttered with
spam, fake digital friends,

00:02:05.796 --> 00:02:09.098
partisan media, ingenious
identity thieves,

00:02:09.098 --> 00:02:13.205
world class Ponzi schemers,
a deception epidemic.

00:02:13.205 --> 00:02:18.041
In short, what one author
calls a post-truth society.

00:02:18.041 --> 00:02:21.791
It's been very confusing
for a long time now.

00:02:24.289 --> 00:02:25.539
What do you do?

00:02:29.478 --> 00:02:31.166
- So let's start with the first thing,

00:02:31.166 --> 00:02:32.638
the one that everyone wants to know,

00:02:32.638 --> 00:02:35.857
which is what is the body
language that indicates lying?

00:02:35.857 --> 00:02:38.428
Now unfortunately, there
really isn't one thing

00:02:38.428 --> 00:02:39.954
that is going to, 100% of the time,

00:02:39.954 --> 00:02:41.507
tell you someone's lying.

00:02:41.507 --> 00:02:43.913
When you're looking to detect lying,

00:02:43.913 --> 00:02:47.078
in terms of body language,
what you're looking for

00:02:47.078 --> 00:02:50.005
are tells of stress with
regards to a particular

00:02:50.005 --> 00:02:51.399
subject or topic.

00:02:51.399 --> 00:02:53.921
And what that means is that
reading stress in the body

00:02:53.921 --> 00:02:57.077
is actually much easier than
reading deception, right?

00:02:57.077 --> 00:02:58.675
Stress comes through in
a lot of different ways

00:02:58.675 --> 00:03:01.266
and you can see it in pacifying behaviors.

00:03:01.266 --> 00:03:03.422
So that might be rubbing of the arm.

00:03:03.422 --> 00:03:05.799
If they're sitting, you can
see people rub their leg.

00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:07.816
You'll see people start
to touch right here,

00:03:07.816 --> 00:03:09.620
that's a pacifying behavior.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:12.238
And quite a few people chew on
their lip, cross their arms,

00:03:12.238 --> 00:03:15.940
purse their lips, give you one of those.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:19.228
All of these things create
a lowered, like trying to

00:03:19.228 --> 00:03:21.280
calm yourself down, response.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.495
And we do that in response to stress.

00:03:24.495 --> 00:03:27.044
Now there's one thing that is classic

00:03:27.044 --> 00:03:29.648
when people feel stressed,
and it's called blocking.

00:03:29.648 --> 00:03:32.163
That means putting
anything between themselves

00:03:32.163 --> 00:03:34.492
and the thing that is causing them stress.

00:03:34.492 --> 00:03:37.889
So the classic lying example of blocking,

00:03:37.889 --> 00:03:39.509
would be this, blocking the mouth,

00:03:39.509 --> 00:03:42.449
could be covering the eyes, or
it could be an object, right?

00:03:42.449 --> 00:03:45.198
Right at the time you're
asking them if they're lying,

00:03:45.198 --> 00:03:47.406
they got to whip their phone
out, or they simply move

00:03:47.406 --> 00:03:50.746
something in between you
and them on the desk.

00:03:50.746 --> 00:03:52.706
Blocking is very common.

00:03:52.706 --> 00:03:54.774
- So what I'm going to do,
is I'm going to show you

00:03:54.774 --> 00:03:56.290
two patterns of deception.

00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:57.653
And then we're going to look
at the hot spots and see

00:03:57.653 --> 00:03:59.132
if we can find them ourselves.

00:03:59.132 --> 00:04:01.632
We're going to start with speech.

00:04:02.535 --> 00:04:04.016
- I want you to listen to me.

00:04:04.016 --> 00:04:05.989
I'm going to say this again.

00:04:05.989 --> 00:04:10.682
I did not have sexual
relations with that woman,

00:04:10.682 --> 00:04:12.341
Miss Lewinsky.

00:04:12.341 --> 00:04:16.508
I never told anybody to lie,
not a single time, never.

00:04:17.583 --> 00:04:19.976
These allegations are false,

00:04:19.976 --> 00:04:23.422
and I need to go back to
work for the American people.

00:04:23.422 --> 00:04:24.255
Thank you.

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:29.412
- Okay, what were the telltale signs?

00:04:29.412 --> 00:04:34.127
Well, first we heard what's
know as a non-contracted denial.

00:04:34.127 --> 00:04:36.741
Studies show that people
who are overdetermined

00:04:36.741 --> 00:04:38.763
in their denial will resort to formal,

00:04:38.763 --> 00:04:40.310
rather than informal language.

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:43.108
We also heard distancing
language, that woman.

00:04:43.108 --> 00:04:45.709
We know that liars will
unconsciously distance themselves

00:04:45.709 --> 00:04:49.853
from their subject using
language as their tool.

00:04:49.853 --> 00:04:53.126
Now, if Bill Clinton had said,
well, to tell you the truth,

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:55.539
or Richard Nixon's
favorite, in all candor,

00:04:55.539 --> 00:04:57.676
it would have been a dead
giveaway for any lie spotter

00:04:57.676 --> 00:04:59.525
that knows qualifying
language, as it's called,

00:04:59.525 --> 00:05:02.639
qualifying language like
that further discredits

00:05:02.639 --> 00:05:04.269
the subject.

00:05:04.269 --> 00:05:06.783
Now, if he had repeated the
question in its entirety,

00:05:06.783 --> 00:05:08.816
or if he had peppered his account with

00:05:08.816 --> 00:05:10.968
a little too much detail,

00:05:10.968 --> 00:05:12.734
and we're all really
glad he didn't do that,

00:05:12.734 --> 00:05:15.216
he would have further discredited himself.

00:05:15.216 --> 00:05:16.468
Freud had it right.

00:05:16.468 --> 00:05:20.589
Freud said, look, there's
much more to it than speech.

00:05:20.589 --> 00:05:22.986
No mortal can keep a secret.

00:05:22.986 --> 00:05:26.392
If his lips are silent, he
chatters with his fingertips.

00:05:26.392 --> 00:05:27.260
And we all do it.

00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:29.358
No matter how powerful
you are, we all chatter

00:05:29.358 --> 00:05:31.092
with our fingertips.

00:05:31.092 --> 00:05:33.214
I'm going to show you
Dominique Strauss-Kahn

00:05:33.214 --> 00:05:37.257
with Obama, who's chattering
with his fingertips.

00:05:37.257 --> 00:05:39.101
(laughter)

00:05:39.101 --> 00:05:42.434
Now, this brings us to our next pattern,

00:05:43.815 --> 00:05:46.404
which is body language.

00:05:46.404 --> 00:05:48.680
With body language,
here's what you got to do.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.586
You really got to just throw
your assumptions out the door.

00:05:51.586 --> 00:05:54.226
Let the science temper your
knowledge a little bit,

00:05:54.226 --> 00:05:56.837
because we think liars
fidget all the time.

00:05:56.837 --> 00:05:57.838
Well guess what?

00:05:57.838 --> 00:05:59.354
They're known to freeze their upper bodies

00:05:59.354 --> 00:06:00.461
when they're lying.

00:06:00.461 --> 00:06:03.141
We think liars won't look you in the eyes.

00:06:03.141 --> 00:06:03.974
Well guess what?

00:06:03.974 --> 00:06:05.420
They look you in the
eyes a little too much,

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:07.649
just to compensate for that myth.

00:06:07.649 --> 00:06:11.450
We think warmth and smiles
convey honesty, sincerity.

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.277
But a trained lie spotter
can spot a fake smile

00:06:14.277 --> 00:06:15.413
a mile away.

00:06:15.413 --> 00:06:19.288
Can you all spot the fake smile here?

00:06:19.288 --> 00:06:24.126
You can consciously contract
the muscles in your cheeks,

00:06:24.126 --> 00:06:25.728
but the real smile's in the eyes.

00:06:25.728 --> 00:06:26.761
The crow's feet of the eyes,

00:06:26.761 --> 00:06:29.610
they cannot be consciously contracted,

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:31.116
especially if you overdid the Botox.

00:06:31.116 --> 00:06:34.086
Don't overdo the Botox, nobody
will think you're honest.

00:06:34.086 --> 00:06:36.328
Clark Freshman is a law
professor who runs a firm

00:06:36.328 --> 00:06:38.308
that trains lawyers and negotiators on

00:06:38.308 --> 00:06:40.294
proper lie detection techniques.

00:06:40.294 --> 00:06:41.958
He says that while
there's no surefire method

00:06:41.958 --> 00:06:44.434
for detecting liars, there
are some advanced methods

00:06:44.434 --> 00:06:46.264
they employ during questioning.

00:06:46.264 --> 00:06:48.967
The unanticipated question
approach, for example,

00:06:48.967 --> 00:06:51.232
is useful in cases where
you suspect someone may be

00:06:51.232 --> 00:06:52.632
trying to cover something up.

00:06:52.632 --> 00:06:54.691
This is based on the idea that
people in these situations

00:06:54.691 --> 00:06:57.082
will often fabricate
stories without considering

00:06:57.082 --> 00:06:58.440
all of the details.

00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:01.118
So if someone commits a
crime, but their alibi says

00:07:01.118 --> 00:07:03.086
they were at a restaurant
when it happened,

00:07:03.086 --> 00:07:05.382
ask them questions that
they wouldn't expect,

00:07:05.382 --> 00:07:07.897
like where was the
restaurant's bathroom located.

00:07:07.897 --> 00:07:09.376
A person who's telling the truth

00:07:09.376 --> 00:07:11.513
will have no problem
answering these details,

00:07:11.513 --> 00:07:13.526
but a liar may struggle.

00:07:13.526 --> 00:07:15.563
- Did you every steal
anything valued at more than

00:07:15.563 --> 00:07:17.252
10, 25 dollars?

00:07:17.252 --> 00:07:18.529
- No.

00:07:18.529 --> 00:07:21.075
- Are you basically a dishonest person?

00:07:21.075 --> 00:07:23.155
- Basically, a dishonest person?

00:07:23.155 --> 00:07:24.406
(sighs)

00:07:24.406 --> 00:07:27.383
- [Reporter] This polygraph
examiner, and retired FBI agent,

00:07:27.383 --> 00:07:30.291
doesn't need a machine to pinpoint a liar.

00:07:30.291 --> 00:07:34.016
- The eyes are a window to the soul.

00:07:34.016 --> 00:07:35.532
You can see right through them.

00:07:35.532 --> 00:07:36.880
- What do they expect from me?

00:07:36.880 --> 00:07:37.713
That's a good question.

00:07:37.713 --> 00:07:39.720
- [Reporter] Take a close
look at this guy's eyes.

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:42.723
Before Jason answers, he
looks up to the right.

00:07:42.723 --> 00:07:45.880
- Looking up to the right means
you're making up something.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:49.380
You're visually picturing
something that's never happened,

00:07:49.380 --> 00:07:50.749
you know, that's a lie.

00:07:50.749 --> 00:07:52.682
- [Reporter] Up to the left,
you're remembering something.

00:07:52.682 --> 00:07:55.727
Only problem, it's the
opposite for lefties,

00:07:55.727 --> 00:07:58.486
like Jason, so he's telling the truth.

00:07:58.486 --> 00:08:01.775
Shifty eyes could also
indicate dishonesty.

00:08:01.775 --> 00:08:03.178
- The moment they complete that lie,

00:08:03.178 --> 00:08:06.215
they look right back at me,
because they're trying to see

00:08:06.215 --> 00:08:08.101
whether I'm buying what they're selling.

00:08:08.101 --> 00:08:09.434
- [Noah Zandan] Liars
typically explain events

00:08:09.434 --> 00:08:11.779
in simple terms since our brains struggle

00:08:11.779 --> 00:08:13.927
to build a complex lie.

00:08:13.927 --> 00:08:16.017
Judgment and evaluation are complex things

00:08:16.017 --> 00:08:17.940
for our brains to compute.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.269
As a U.S. President
once famously insisted,

00:08:20.269 --> 00:08:23.378
I did not have sexual
relations with that women.

00:08:23.378 --> 00:08:26.172
And finally, even though liars
keep descriptions simple,

00:08:26.172 --> 00:08:28.350
they tend to use longer
and more convoluted

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:31.122
sentence structure,
inserting unnecessary words

00:08:31.122 --> 00:08:33.403
and irrelevant, but
factual sounding details,

00:08:33.403 --> 00:08:35.286
in order to pad the lie.

00:08:35.286 --> 00:08:37.565
Another president confronted
with a scandal proclaimed,

00:08:37.565 --> 00:08:40.418
I can say categorically
that this investigation

00:08:40.418 --> 00:08:42.928
indicates that no one on
the White House staff,

00:08:42.928 --> 00:08:45.395
no one in this administration
presently employed

00:08:45.395 --> 00:08:48.476
was involved in this
very bizarre incident.

00:08:48.476 --> 00:08:51.859
Let's apply linguistic analysis
to some famous examples.

00:08:51.859 --> 00:08:55.082
Take seven-time Tour de
France winner Lance Armstrong.

00:08:55.082 --> 00:08:57.768
When comparing a 2005 interview
in which he had denied

00:08:57.768 --> 00:08:59.955
taking performance enhancing drugs,

00:08:59.955 --> 00:09:02.873
to a 2013 interview in
which he admitted it,

00:09:02.873 --> 00:09:06.852
his use of personal pronouns
increased by nearly 3/4.

00:09:06.852 --> 00:09:09.514
Note the contrast between
the following two quotes.

00:09:09.514 --> 00:09:12.454
First, okay, you know, a guy in a French,

00:09:12.454 --> 00:09:14.984
in a Parisian laboratory,
opens up your sample,

00:09:14.984 --> 00:09:17.994
you know, John-Francis so
and so, and he tests it,

00:09:17.994 --> 00:09:20.014
and then you get a phone
call from a newspaper

00:09:20.014 --> 00:09:24.932
that says we found you to be
positive six times for EPO.

00:09:24.932 --> 00:09:27.124
Second, I lost myself in all of that.

00:09:27.124 --> 00:09:28.784
I'm sure there would be
other people that couldn't

00:09:28.784 --> 00:09:31.401
handle it, but I certainly
couldn't handle it,

00:09:31.401 --> 00:09:34.367
and I was used to controlling
everything in my life.

00:09:34.367 --> 00:09:36.959
I controlled every outcome in my life.

00:09:36.959 --> 00:09:40.192
In his denial, Armstrong
described a hypothetical situation

00:09:40.192 --> 00:09:42.546
focused on someone else, removing himself

00:09:42.546 --> 00:09:44.673
from the situation entirely.

00:09:44.673 --> 00:09:46.665
In his admission, he owns his statements,

00:09:46.665 --> 00:09:50.366
delving into his personal
emotions and motivations.

00:09:50.366 --> 00:09:51.688
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:09:51.688 --> 00:09:53.337
I'd love to know what did you think?

00:09:53.337 --> 00:09:54.481
Did you enjoy this video?

00:09:54.481 --> 00:09:55.637
Did you learn something new?

00:09:55.637 --> 00:09:57.849
Are you going to apply
this somehow to your life

00:09:57.849 --> 00:09:59.003
or your business?

00:09:59.003 --> 00:10:00.598
Is there a liar that you want to check out

00:10:00.598 --> 00:10:03.017
